            <ArticleTitle>[Abdominal pain at the emergency service. Follow-up of patients with pain of unknown origin].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>23-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>450 patients who arrived at the emergency department with abdominal pain were studied. 71% arrived without having previously consulted another doctor. The most frequent diagnosis was reno ureteral pain, non-specific pain, gastroenteritis and in geriatric patients (hernia, biliar pathology) other pathology. 69 patients with non-specific pain were followed-up during a period of one year. The symptoms returned in 40% of patients. Only 3% required urgent medical treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
